# Feature registry discipline

`app/core/ai/features/registry.py:FeatureRegistry` is the source of truth.

## Rules

1. A feature is a pure function: `(pl.DataFrame) -> pl.Series | pl.DataFrame`.
   No I/O. No global state. No randomness without an explicit seed parameter.
2. Register with `(name, version, fn)`. The registry computes a `body_hash`
   from the function bytecode. If `(name, version)` already exists with a
   different body_hash, registration raises — bump the version instead of
   silently mutating.
3. Models declare their inputs as a list of `(name, version)` tuples (see
   `app/core/ai/features/feature_sets.py`). Training uses `build_set` to
   materialise exactly that combination.
4. Output column names from `build_set` are prefixed `<name>__v<version>__` so
   the model's training and inference schemas are byte-identical.
5. Features must NOT call models. Features compute primitives; the model
   composes them.

## When you add a feature

- Pick a stable name (snake_case, descriptive, no abbreviations the next
  person won't recognise).
- Start at `version=1`. If you need to change the math later, register
  `version=2`; old models keep using `v1`.
- Add a unit test with synthetic input that pins the expected output of the
  first 5 rows.
